Window frames are often treated as an afterthought, yet they account for a large share of heat loss in a well-insulated house. In cold climates the frame material affects comfort, condensation risk and long-term maintenance as much as the glass does. Timber frames offer good thermal performance and a warm appearance, but they need periodic refinishing, especially on south and west elevations where sun exposure is strongest.

{INJECTION}

uPVC frames are the budget choice and perform well when they include multiple internal chambers, although wide profiles can look heavy on smaller openings. Aluminium-clad timber combines a durable exterior skin with a timber interior, which makes it popular for coastal sites and for clients who want low maintenance without giving up a natural finish indoors. Whatever material you choose, ask suppliers for the whole-window U-value rather than the centre-of-glass figure, check the installation detail for the airtightness tape, and confirm lead times early, since triple-glazed units can take ten weeks or more to arrive on site.
